WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:22.039
And speaking of VSEPR, that's
what we did last lecture.

00:00:22.039 --> 00:00:23.741
We're going to move on today.

00:00:23.741 --> 00:00:26.276
But I want to just clarify
something about VSEPR.

00:00:28.879 --> 00:00:31.448
When I was talking
about this I tried

00:00:31.448 --> 00:00:33.617
to put two different
concepts in the same place.

00:00:33.617 --> 00:00:36.286
And I put something in the table
that shouldn't have been there.

00:00:36.286 --> 00:00:38.188
And people corrected me.

00:00:38.188 --> 00:00:41.892
I want to be sure that there
is no confusion around this.

00:00:41.892 --> 00:00:48.298
So what we had was a
case of formaldehyde.

00:00:48.298 --> 00:00:54.071
And we had the case of SO2.

00:01:00.044 --> 00:01:04.848
And I want to be really
clear about this.

00:01:04.848 --> 00:01:07.918
Both of these
molecules, when you're

00:01:07.918 --> 00:01:15.292
thinking about VSEPR, the number
of electron domain count--

00:01:15.292 --> 00:01:15.793
Right.

00:01:15.793 --> 00:01:17.628
Remember, that's how we start.

00:01:17.628 --> 00:01:24.435
The number of electron domains
is the same for both of them.

00:01:24.435 --> 00:01:28.605
And we did this on Wednesday.

00:01:28.605 --> 00:01:29.106
Right.

00:01:29.106 --> 00:01:31.608
And if the number of
electron domains is the same,

00:01:31.608 --> 00:01:35.946
then the electron domain
geometry is trigonal planar.

00:01:35.946 --> 00:01:37.314
Right.

00:01:37.314 --> 00:01:38.549
OK.

00:01:38.549 --> 00:01:47.591
But I was also telling
you about repulsion order

00:01:47.591 --> 00:01:49.960
at the same time.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:53.097
And so I put the wrong
thing in the table.

00:01:53.097 --> 00:01:53.997
And then I erased it.

00:01:53.997 --> 00:01:55.732
And I just want to
be sure we're clear

00:01:55.732 --> 00:01:57.801
and we don't get bent
out of shape about it.

00:01:57.801 --> 00:01:58.502
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:58.502 --> 00:01:59.002
Stop.

00:01:59.002 --> 00:01:59.503
[CLAPPING]

00:01:59.503 --> 00:02:00.370
Get out of here.

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:01.438
Stop.

00:02:01.438 --> 00:02:02.005
Stop it.

00:02:02.005 --> 00:02:02.506
All right.

00:02:02.506 --> 00:02:03.907
All right.

00:02:03.907 --> 00:02:05.576
I'm going to still be here.

00:02:05.576 --> 00:02:06.710
I'm going to be here.

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:07.377
All right.

00:02:07.377 --> 00:02:09.713
So like here, right, the shape.

00:02:13.684 --> 00:02:21.925
Remember, what the
table was about

00:02:21.925 --> 00:02:26.163
was the number of lone pairs.

00:02:26.163 --> 00:02:27.164
OK.

00:02:27.164 --> 00:02:28.532
The number of lone pairs.

00:02:28.532 --> 00:02:30.634
And so if I've got
the three binding

00:02:30.634 --> 00:02:33.270
pairs and no lone
pairs, then it's

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:35.439
going to be a
trigonal planar shape.

00:02:35.439 --> 00:02:38.742
So that is trigonal planar.

00:02:38.742 --> 00:02:42.478
And over here I've
got two bonding pairs

00:02:42.478 --> 00:02:44.548
and one lone pair.

00:02:44.548 --> 00:02:47.284
And so this shape
is called bent.

00:02:47.284 --> 00:02:49.553
Now, what I did is
I used that word.

00:02:49.553 --> 00:02:51.221
Aw, why?

00:02:51.221 --> 00:02:51.788
I don't know.

00:02:51.788 --> 00:02:53.290
But you make mistakes.

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:56.160
When you're in the
arena, you keep going.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.262
And if you make a
mistake, you admit it.

00:02:58.262 --> 00:03:00.197
You leave it all there.

00:03:00.197 --> 00:03:00.964
You do your best.

00:03:00.964 --> 00:03:01.732
Forget the rest.

00:03:01.732 --> 00:03:03.634
That's all you can ever do.

00:03:03.634 --> 00:03:05.135
Now, here's the thing.

00:03:05.135 --> 00:03:06.470
I said bent.

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:07.538
I said the word bent.

00:03:07.538 --> 00:03:09.339
And what I was
talking about was how

00:03:09.339 --> 00:03:12.643
that bends a little bit
because of the repulsion order.

00:03:12.643 --> 00:03:13.177
Yeah.

00:03:13.177 --> 00:03:15.512
But that shape is not bent.

00:03:15.512 --> 00:03:17.214
I want to be real
clear about that.

00:03:17.214 --> 00:03:19.049
That shape is trigonal planer.

00:03:19.049 --> 00:03:21.418
And it's a little bit distorted.

00:03:21.418 --> 00:03:23.453
OK.

00:03:23.453 --> 00:03:26.456
This shape is bent.

00:03:26.456 --> 00:03:30.427
That shape is bent, because
it's got a lone pair.

00:03:30.427 --> 00:03:33.463
Remember, the shape is
about where the atoms are.

00:03:33.463 --> 00:03:37.201
But it's also a
little bit distorted.

00:03:37.201 --> 00:03:38.235
Right?

00:03:38.235 --> 00:03:43.240
Because the lone pair pushes on
those guys a little bit more.

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:43.807
All right.

00:03:43.807 --> 00:03:44.308
OK.

00:03:44.308 --> 00:03:48.712
So those are the two concepts
that I was saying all at once.

00:03:48.712 --> 00:03:50.614
And I want to make sure
we understand them.

00:03:50.614 --> 00:03:52.349
There's trigonal planar.

00:03:52.349 --> 00:03:53.350
there's bent.

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:54.751
And then there's
repulsion order,

00:03:54.751 --> 00:03:58.188
which talks about little changes
in the angle from those parent

00:03:58.188 --> 00:03:58.755
shapes.

00:03:58.755 --> 00:03:59.256
OK.

00:03:59.256 --> 00:04:00.023
Good.

00:04:00.023 --> 00:04:00.524
All right.

00:04:00.524 --> 00:04:02.926
Now, onto today.

00:04:02.926 --> 00:04:03.994
Now, here's the thing.

00:04:03.994 --> 00:04:08.799
Now, in all of this
stuff with Lewis,

00:04:08.799 --> 00:04:12.002
it's a really good way
to think about molecules.

00:04:12.002 --> 00:04:16.373
But this tells me that
there's electrons on the bond.

00:04:16.373 --> 00:04:17.808
It tells me that
there's electrons

00:04:17.808 --> 00:04:21.111
really localized in the
region between the atoms.

00:04:21.111 --> 00:04:25.082
But see, the thing is,
how much is it localized?

00:04:25.082 --> 00:04:26.650
How much is it really?

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:31.221
Are these electrons-- if I were
to draw the two electrons, are

00:04:31.221 --> 00:04:34.424
those dots right there?

00:04:34.424 --> 00:04:38.428
It doesn't really tell me
more information than that.

00:04:38.428 --> 00:04:42.099
But if we remember the picture--
let's see if I can pull.

00:04:42.099 --> 00:04:42.599
Yep.

00:04:42.599 --> 00:04:43.467
There it is.

00:04:43.467 --> 00:04:45.869
There's a picture I
showed you when we first

00:04:45.869 --> 00:04:47.838
talked about covalent bonds.

00:04:47.838 --> 00:04:49.606
Sharing is caring.

00:04:49.606 --> 00:04:50.907
Remember what happens.

00:04:50.907 --> 00:04:52.042
I've got the two protons.

00:04:52.042 --> 00:04:53.143
And we talked about these.

00:04:53.143 --> 00:04:54.444
A repulsion.

00:04:54.444 --> 00:04:55.579
And then the two electrons.

00:04:55.579 --> 00:04:56.680
Repulsion.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:58.282
And then I drew for
you the fact that,

00:04:58.282 --> 00:04:59.549
but they can come together.

00:04:59.549 --> 00:05:01.018
And they can be
like, you know what?

00:05:01.018 --> 00:05:03.420
You can use my proton for a
little of your attraction.

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:05.355
And hey, can I use your--

00:05:05.355 --> 00:05:06.223
And they share it.

00:05:06.223 --> 00:05:08.825
And each electron
sees both of them.

00:05:08.825 --> 00:05:12.663
And this is where
those electrons are.

00:05:12.663 --> 00:05:13.363
But look at that.

00:05:13.363 --> 00:05:15.232
The proton is there and there.

00:05:15.232 --> 00:05:18.468
And the electron is in
this probability cloud

00:05:18.468 --> 00:05:19.703
around both of them.

00:05:19.703 --> 00:05:24.741
That's the molecular orbital.

00:05:24.741 --> 00:05:26.443
And Lewis can't
tell us about that.

00:05:26.443 --> 00:05:27.544
So we need something else.

00:05:27.544 --> 00:05:29.446
And that's what
today's topic is about.

00:05:29.446 --> 00:05:32.115
Today we're going to talk
about something called

00:05:32.115 --> 00:05:35.886
molecular orbital theory
that gives us a sense of how

00:05:35.886 --> 00:05:40.991
these atomic orbitals overlap.

00:05:40.991 --> 00:05:45.696
It gives us a much better
and a deeper description

00:05:45.696 --> 00:05:48.899
with more details about
these covalent bonds.

00:05:48.899 --> 00:05:49.499
OK.

00:05:49.499 --> 00:05:50.000
All right.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:55.672
Now, so let's write a few things
about molecular orbital theory.

00:05:55.672 --> 00:05:58.241
Molecular orbital.

00:05:58.241 --> 00:06:03.146
And what we're going to do is
say, MO, and Molecular Orbital

00:06:03.146 --> 00:06:03.647
theory.

00:06:03.647 --> 00:06:06.149
So we'll keep a list of
some important things.

00:06:06.149 --> 00:06:06.650
All right.

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:08.719
Well, first of all,
the first thing

00:06:08.719 --> 00:06:17.494
is that atomic orbitals,
or, as we may call them,

00:06:17.494 --> 00:06:25.902
AOs, are the basis for--

00:06:25.902 --> 00:06:28.438
watch this efficiency, the MOs.

00:06:28.438 --> 00:06:29.773
I didn't have to write it again.

00:06:29.773 --> 00:06:30.607
Right?

00:06:30.607 --> 00:06:31.108
OK.

00:06:31.108 --> 00:06:32.075
So what does that mean?

00:06:32.075 --> 00:06:36.813
Well, it means that I'm going to
construct the MOs from the AOs.

00:06:36.813 --> 00:06:38.181
Now, the AOs are
the things we've

00:06:38.181 --> 00:06:39.816
been playing with and making.

00:06:39.816 --> 00:06:40.317
Right?

00:06:40.317 --> 00:06:41.952
We solved the
Schrodinger equation.

00:06:41.952 --> 00:06:49.726
So the MOs are going to
be linear combinations.

00:06:49.726 --> 00:06:50.227
All right.

00:06:50.227 --> 00:06:56.332
Combinations of the AOs.

00:06:56.332 --> 00:07:02.773
And that's why you might
see this, in fact, called

00:07:02.773 --> 00:07:04.307
LCAO theory.

00:07:04.307 --> 00:07:05.308
LCAO.

00:07:05.308 --> 00:07:08.612
Linear Combination
of Atomic Orbitals.

00:07:08.612 --> 00:07:10.180
OK.

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:19.589
And in particular, sum
and difference of orbitals

00:07:19.589 --> 00:07:20.424
is what we're about.

00:07:20.424 --> 00:07:25.095
Because this is like what
we talked about before.

00:07:25.095 --> 00:07:26.963
These are waves.

00:07:26.963 --> 00:07:27.464
Right.

00:07:27.464 --> 00:07:28.832
These are waves.

00:07:28.832 --> 00:07:34.638
Waves can either constructively
or destructively interfere.

00:07:34.638 --> 00:07:37.407
And that's what we're going
to explore in MO theory.

00:07:37.407 --> 00:07:41.845
By adding or subtracting
these wave functions,

00:07:41.845 --> 00:07:46.016
these orbitals, these
atomic orbitals.

00:07:46.016 --> 00:07:47.082
So that's enough to start.

00:07:47.082 --> 00:07:47.784
Let's see.

00:07:47.784 --> 00:07:49.953
So look, basis.

00:07:49.953 --> 00:07:52.522
A basis is nothing
more than the things

00:07:52.522 --> 00:07:55.292
that I'm going to use to
represent the things that I

00:07:55.292 --> 00:07:56.659
want.

00:07:56.659 --> 00:07:57.159
Right.

00:07:57.159 --> 00:07:59.596
And the things that I want
are these molecular orbitals.

00:07:59.596 --> 00:08:01.898
And the things that I'm going
to use to represent those

00:08:01.898 --> 00:08:06.503
are these, these
beautiful atomic orbitals

00:08:06.503 --> 00:08:09.606
that we spent a lot of time
understanding and developing.

00:08:09.606 --> 00:08:15.412
We'll start with the
simplest case, which is--

00:08:15.412 --> 00:08:17.147
I think I have--

00:08:17.147 --> 00:08:18.448
oh, I even highlighted it.

00:08:18.448 --> 00:08:21.685
And I said, let's first
make molecular orbitals

00:08:21.685 --> 00:08:25.722
using combinations
of the S orbital.

00:08:25.722 --> 00:08:26.790
That's the S orbital.

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:28.892
That's the 1s orbital up there.

00:08:28.892 --> 00:08:32.962
Remember, by the
way, blue and orange

00:08:32.962 --> 00:08:34.798
is just the sign of the wave.

00:08:34.798 --> 00:08:35.432
Right.

00:08:35.432 --> 00:08:37.467
This is not a charge.

00:08:37.467 --> 00:08:40.135
Please don't make
that confusion.

00:08:40.135 --> 00:08:41.337
These are just waves.

00:08:41.337 --> 00:08:41.837
Right.

00:08:41.837 --> 00:08:43.440
Waves.

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:45.809
And let's go back and see it.

00:08:45.809 --> 00:08:48.144
The p orbital has
a plus and a minus.

00:08:48.144 --> 00:08:49.412
It's a wave.

00:08:49.412 --> 00:08:50.380
All right.

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:52.582
And the 2s orbital has that too.

00:08:52.582 --> 00:08:55.151
And then you square it, and
you get the probabilities.

00:08:55.151 --> 00:08:56.520
Right?

00:08:56.520 --> 00:08:57.354
OK.

00:08:57.354 --> 00:09:00.490
So we'll start with 1s where
it's all just one sign.

00:09:00.490 --> 00:09:02.559
And I can add it.

00:09:02.559 --> 00:09:05.462
I can add an S and another
S. Or I can subtract it.

00:09:05.462 --> 00:09:06.329
So let's do that.

00:09:06.329 --> 00:09:06.830
Right.

00:09:06.830 --> 00:09:08.498
So we'll do that over here.

00:09:08.498 --> 00:09:08.999
All right.

00:09:13.203 --> 00:09:21.478
So if I take a 1s orbital, and
I add it to another 1s orbital,

00:09:21.478 --> 00:09:24.848
well, you can see, as I
bring these close together,

00:09:24.848 --> 00:09:26.983
if they have the same
sign, these are waves.

00:09:26.983 --> 00:09:29.319
So if the waves
are both positive,

00:09:29.319 --> 00:09:32.956
and I bring them together, they
can constructively interfere.

00:09:32.956 --> 00:09:35.592
So as I bring them
closer and closer,

00:09:35.592 --> 00:09:37.294
well, I might actually
get the picture

00:09:37.294 --> 00:09:38.628
that I showed you, right?

00:09:38.628 --> 00:09:42.165
I might actually get something
that looks like this.

00:09:45.068 --> 00:09:45.735
OK.

00:09:45.735 --> 00:09:47.470
I might actually get
something like this.

00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:55.545
Now, if I subtracted them,
then as I bring these orbitals

00:09:55.545 --> 00:10:00.550
closer together, well, they're
going to cancel each other.

00:10:00.550 --> 00:10:03.820
And you can see right here,
if I bring these orbitals--

00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:06.423
by the way, which are coming
from two different atoms.

00:10:06.423 --> 00:10:06.923
Right.

00:10:06.923 --> 00:10:11.828
The whole point is we're talking
about molecules, molecular.

00:10:11.828 --> 00:10:13.897
So if I bring them
together now like this,

00:10:13.897 --> 00:10:17.367
but I'm subtracting
these orbitals,

00:10:17.367 --> 00:10:19.469
then you can see that right
at the interface, right

00:10:19.469 --> 00:10:22.205
between them, it's always zero.

00:10:22.205 --> 00:10:24.074
Because I've just
subtracted the same amount.

00:10:24.074 --> 00:10:25.976
No matter how close
they get, it's zero.

00:10:25.976 --> 00:10:27.477
That's a node.

00:10:27.477 --> 00:10:27.978
Right.

00:10:27.978 --> 00:10:29.846
And, in fact, what
you're going to get

00:10:29.846 --> 00:10:31.982
is something that
looks like this.

00:10:35.251 --> 00:10:40.023
Now, so what I've done is
I have added and subtracted

00:10:40.023 --> 00:10:42.525
the same AO.

00:10:42.525 --> 00:10:44.861
And I've created two MOs.

00:10:44.861 --> 00:10:46.596
So these are my MOs.

00:10:46.596 --> 00:10:49.132
And these are my starting AOs.

00:10:49.132 --> 00:10:53.503
That's about the simplest
thing you can do in MO theory.

00:10:53.503 --> 00:10:55.238
So I'm using these as a basis.

00:10:55.238 --> 00:10:58.942
Now, there's something very
important, which is that--

00:10:58.942 --> 00:11:01.111
I kind of already
alluded to this--

00:11:01.111 --> 00:11:04.547
these are two atoms coming
together to make a bond.

00:11:04.547 --> 00:11:07.117
This is the bonding axis.

00:11:07.117 --> 00:11:08.318
This is the bonding axis.

00:11:08.318 --> 00:11:08.985
OK.

00:11:08.985 --> 00:11:15.425
Now, if an orbital,
if an MO is symmetric,

00:11:15.425 --> 00:11:24.634
so if it has cylindrical
symmetry about that bonding

00:11:24.634 --> 00:11:36.680
axis, then it's called
a sigma orbital.

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:37.847
It's called a sigma orbital.

00:11:37.847 --> 00:11:40.383
That's the MO.

00:11:40.383 --> 00:11:42.452
Sigma is a notation.

00:11:42.452 --> 00:11:44.621
A sigma orbital is
one that has symmetry

00:11:44.621 --> 00:11:51.094
around the internuclear
axis, the two--

00:11:51.094 --> 00:11:54.497
Does Pauli exclusion
not apply to that?

00:11:54.497 --> 00:11:58.201
We're coming to that very soon.

00:11:58.201 --> 00:11:59.703
Because these are
just the orbitals.

00:11:59.703 --> 00:12:01.037
I got to do something with them.

00:12:01.037 --> 00:12:02.238
It's just like over here.

00:12:02.238 --> 00:12:03.339
I made all these orbitals.

00:12:03.339 --> 00:12:06.743
But then I had to fill them.

00:12:06.743 --> 00:12:08.978
That got me excited.

00:12:08.978 --> 00:12:11.681
But you can see right here.

00:12:11.681 --> 00:12:17.454
When I add them up
there's charge in between.

00:12:17.454 --> 00:12:19.956
So this looks like it's
going to have a bond.

00:12:24.394 --> 00:12:26.863
That's a bonding MO.

00:12:26.863 --> 00:12:30.333
But if I subtracted them,
this doesn't look very happy

00:12:30.333 --> 00:12:33.269
in terms of electrons
being in between

00:12:33.269 --> 00:12:35.672
and the caring is sharing
principle of a covalent bond.

00:12:35.672 --> 00:12:36.873
Right.

00:12:36.873 --> 00:12:39.776
So if I were to put an
electron in this MO,

00:12:39.776 --> 00:12:40.844
it doesn't look bonding.

00:12:40.844 --> 00:12:42.445
In fact, it looks--

00:12:42.445 --> 00:12:43.346
Non-bonding.

00:12:43.346 --> 00:12:45.548
Or antibonding.

00:12:45.548 --> 00:12:47.383
It's actually not
quite non-binding.

00:12:47.383 --> 00:12:48.651
We'll get to that later.

00:12:48.651 --> 00:12:51.755
This would be antibonding.

00:12:51.755 --> 00:12:53.156
Why?

00:12:53.156 --> 00:12:57.127
Because there's not even
a chance of electron

00:12:57.127 --> 00:12:58.795
density in between.

00:12:58.795 --> 00:13:01.998
That's your starting
principle of the relationship.

00:13:01.998 --> 00:13:02.799
Let's try to share.

00:13:02.799 --> 00:13:06.803
Oh, by the way, right
half way, no chance.

00:13:06.803 --> 00:13:08.371
That's not a very
good way to start.

00:13:08.371 --> 00:13:10.807
And you can see why
this is antibonding.

00:13:10.807 --> 00:13:12.542
It's the opposite.

00:13:12.542 --> 00:13:14.577
You're actually pushing
charge away from the bond

00:13:14.577 --> 00:13:17.714
if you're an electron
in that orbital.

00:13:17.714 --> 00:13:19.415
So that's an
antibonding orbital.

00:13:19.415 --> 00:13:21.551
Now we get to the question.

00:13:21.551 --> 00:13:23.019
What do you do with
these orbitals?

00:13:23.019 --> 00:13:25.088
Well, you make them.

00:13:25.088 --> 00:13:28.992
I made these with 1s.

00:13:28.992 --> 00:13:32.962
And the way that we
typically draw this is we

00:13:32.962 --> 00:13:35.932
go back to the same
picture that we've

00:13:35.932 --> 00:13:41.171
been working with,
1s here and 1s here.

00:13:41.171 --> 00:13:42.739
And I brought those
orbitals together,

00:13:42.739 --> 00:13:47.710
and I formed one that lowered
the energy of the system,

00:13:47.710 --> 00:13:51.347
and another that actually
raised the energy of the system,

00:13:51.347 --> 00:13:53.449
because the electrons
are repelling.

00:13:53.449 --> 00:13:55.552
They're actually less bonding.

00:13:55.552 --> 00:13:56.786
They're antibonding.

00:13:56.786 --> 00:13:57.287
All right.

00:14:00.924 --> 00:14:04.127
So these are my MOs.

00:14:04.127 --> 00:14:05.728
Those are my AOs.

00:14:05.728 --> 00:14:06.229
Right.

00:14:06.229 --> 00:14:07.730
AO, AO.

00:14:07.730 --> 00:14:11.868
And now, this is
called a sigma orbital.

00:14:11.868 --> 00:14:14.437
And the way we refer to
the antibonding orbital

00:14:14.437 --> 00:14:17.040
is with a little star.

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:20.276
They're both sigma because
they both satisfy that.

00:14:20.276 --> 00:14:20.777
Right.

00:14:20.777 --> 00:14:23.213
They're both sigma
cause they both have

00:14:23.213 --> 00:14:26.916
symmetry around the inter
nuclear bonding axis.

00:14:26.916 --> 00:14:28.251
But now, see, OK.

00:14:28.251 --> 00:14:29.018
So that's good.

00:14:32.555 --> 00:14:33.923
But now let's fill them.

00:14:33.923 --> 00:14:35.191
Let's fill them.

00:14:35.191 --> 00:14:39.429
Because now let's
write down the rules.

00:14:39.429 --> 00:14:41.364
And then we'll fill
them for some examples.

00:14:41.364 --> 00:14:41.998
OK.

00:14:41.998 --> 00:14:44.868
So I'm going to keep going
on my MO theory list.

00:14:44.868 --> 00:14:45.368
Right.

00:14:45.368 --> 00:14:49.105
As you can see, when I do it
this way, the number of MOs

00:14:49.105 --> 00:14:56.713
equals the number of
AOs used to create them.

00:14:56.713 --> 00:14:59.515
So that's one thing that
we can see right away.

00:14:59.515 --> 00:15:00.383
Right.

00:15:00.383 --> 00:15:01.751
I used two AOs.

00:15:01.751 --> 00:15:02.919
I added and subtracted them.

00:15:02.919 --> 00:15:03.987
I got two MOs.

00:15:03.987 --> 00:15:05.088
That's good.

00:15:05.088 --> 00:15:16.499
Well, another thing is, each
MO will be just like an AO.

00:15:16.499 --> 00:15:17.934
It makes a lot of sense.

00:15:17.934 --> 00:15:20.436
I had room for two
electrons here.

00:15:20.436 --> 00:15:21.838
I had room for two
electrons here.

00:15:21.838 --> 00:15:24.040
I got to have room for four.

00:15:24.040 --> 00:15:24.874
Two here.

00:15:24.874 --> 00:15:25.942
Two here.

00:15:25.942 --> 00:15:26.442
Right.

00:15:26.442 --> 00:15:33.349
So each MO has a max
of two electrons.

00:15:33.349 --> 00:15:39.555
And they must obey
Pauli, just like in AOs.

00:15:39.555 --> 00:15:41.124
They must obey Pauli.

00:15:41.124 --> 00:15:42.725
You can't mess with
quantum mechanics.

00:15:42.725 --> 00:15:43.226
Uh-uh.

00:15:45.395 --> 00:15:46.396
They're still electrons.

00:15:46.396 --> 00:15:48.564
They're still
quantum mechanical.

00:15:48.564 --> 00:15:49.165
All right.

00:15:49.165 --> 00:15:52.869
You're just making their wave
function more sophisticated.

00:15:52.869 --> 00:15:54.737
But they still have to
follow the Schrodinger

00:15:54.737 --> 00:15:57.573
equation and the principles
of quantum mechanics.

00:15:57.573 --> 00:15:59.542
And so, finally, what
we're going to do

00:15:59.542 --> 00:16:01.077
is we're going to fill them.

00:16:01.077 --> 00:16:08.284
So we're going to fill the
MOs with electrons, starting

00:16:08.284 --> 00:16:09.953
with the lowest energy,
just like we did

00:16:09.953 --> 00:16:14.857
for atoms, lowest energy first.

00:16:14.857 --> 00:16:16.125
OK.

00:16:16.125 --> 00:16:17.360
And one more thing.

00:16:17.360 --> 00:16:22.932
Just like we did with atoms
is we're going to obey Hund.

00:16:22.932 --> 00:16:23.766
Hund.

00:16:23.766 --> 00:16:25.868
Hund's rule, which
tells you about how

00:16:25.868 --> 00:16:27.937
to fill degenerate orbitals.

00:16:27.937 --> 00:16:30.974
Well, we're going to have
degenerate orbitals here too.

00:16:30.974 --> 00:16:31.708
We don't yet.

00:16:31.708 --> 00:16:32.508
But we will.

00:16:32.508 --> 00:16:33.676
So we're going to fill them.

00:16:33.676 --> 00:16:36.746
So let's fill them for
the very simplest cases.

00:16:36.746 --> 00:16:37.246
OK.

00:16:37.246 --> 00:16:39.549
So we'll do some cases together.

00:16:39.549 --> 00:16:41.584
And we'll see how that goes.

00:16:41.584 --> 00:16:42.085
All right.

00:16:42.085 --> 00:16:45.788
So what I'm going to do are four
cases just so we can compare.

00:16:45.788 --> 00:16:48.658
So first, we're
going to do H2 plus.

00:16:48.658 --> 00:16:49.158
OK.

00:16:49.158 --> 00:16:50.860
That's the first one.

00:16:50.860 --> 00:16:57.266
And here, what we have
is the same graph.

00:16:57.266 --> 00:17:00.003
But now, over here I
actually can label it now.

00:17:00.003 --> 00:17:01.537
This was just an orbital.

00:17:01.537 --> 00:17:02.638
But now, gazuntite.

00:17:02.638 --> 00:17:04.640
Now, I'll say, OK.

00:17:04.640 --> 00:17:12.080
Well, this would be
like H and H plus.

00:17:12.080 --> 00:17:12.582
OK.

00:17:12.582 --> 00:17:18.121
These are my MOs, sigma,
sigma star, 1s, 1s.

00:17:18.121 --> 00:17:20.823
Oh, boy.

00:17:20.823 --> 00:17:23.626
So if that's H
and that's H plus,

00:17:23.626 --> 00:17:25.828
and they're coming together
to make H2 plus-- that's

00:17:25.828 --> 00:17:26.963
what's in the middle there.

00:17:26.963 --> 00:17:28.330
That's the molecule.

00:17:28.330 --> 00:17:29.065
All right.

00:17:29.065 --> 00:17:33.036
Well, then I also can now
fill in the starting AOs.

00:17:33.036 --> 00:17:33.870
So let's do that.

00:17:36.406 --> 00:17:37.206
That's it.

00:17:37.206 --> 00:17:41.344
Because somebody had to lose
an electron to start with.

00:17:41.344 --> 00:17:43.913
So now, when these come
together, you fill.

00:17:43.913 --> 00:17:45.982
You obey the Pauli exclusion.

00:17:45.982 --> 00:17:47.984
You fill it from the
lowest energy first.

00:17:47.984 --> 00:17:50.086
You say, well, I only
got one electron.

00:17:50.086 --> 00:17:51.721
There it is.

00:17:51.721 --> 00:17:56.659
That's my MO diagram
populated by electrons.

00:17:56.659 --> 00:17:58.561
OK.

00:17:58.561 --> 00:18:00.963
Now, there's a principle
that comes out of this.

00:18:00.963 --> 00:18:04.767
There's a way of understanding
bond strength now.

00:18:04.767 --> 00:18:06.636
There's a way of
understanding bond strength.

00:18:06.636 --> 00:18:09.539
And that is a very
important concept.

00:18:09.539 --> 00:18:13.176
Now, let's put it
here in our list.

00:18:13.176 --> 00:18:14.277
OK.

00:18:14.277 --> 00:18:16.045
And it's a concept
called bond order.

00:18:18.648 --> 00:18:21.517
Now, this one I really
want to write BO.

00:18:21.517 --> 00:18:24.253
But I don't know if
that's a good idea.

00:18:27.356 --> 00:18:28.591
I got Laura on that one.

00:18:28.591 --> 00:18:29.725
OK.

00:18:29.725 --> 00:18:31.861
And there's a
definition for this.

00:18:31.861 --> 00:18:37.900
It's going to equal 1/2
of the number of electrons

00:18:37.900 --> 00:18:48.010
in a bonding orbital, minus
the number of electrons

00:18:48.010 --> 00:18:53.883
in an antibonding orbital.

00:18:53.883 --> 00:18:54.383
Sorry.

00:18:54.383 --> 00:18:57.954
This is a little bit small here.

00:18:57.954 --> 00:19:00.456
But we'll do a bunch of them
so you can see what this means.

00:19:00.456 --> 00:19:00.957
Right.

00:19:00.957 --> 00:19:03.593
So now, the bond order is
important because the bond

00:19:03.593 --> 00:19:21.244
order of a molecule, if it's
higher than a stronger bond,

00:19:21.244 --> 00:19:29.285
and it has to be greater
than zero for stability.

00:19:29.285 --> 00:19:31.754
Let's see how that works.

00:19:31.754 --> 00:19:35.391
And we'll see this intuitively
as well as we fill these up.

00:19:35.391 --> 00:19:35.892
Right.

00:19:35.892 --> 00:19:38.461
So if I count the bond order
here-- oh, I'm going to do it.

00:19:38.461 --> 00:19:40.163
I don't feel like
writing it out.

00:19:40.163 --> 00:19:46.869
The BO on this one is 1/2 times
1 minus 0, which equals 1/2.

00:19:46.869 --> 00:19:47.370
OK.

00:19:47.370 --> 00:19:48.404
Good.

00:19:48.404 --> 00:19:49.238
It's 1/2.

00:19:49.238 --> 00:19:50.873
Well, it's greater than 0.

00:19:50.873 --> 00:19:53.576
So right away I know that the
H2 plus molecule is probably

00:19:53.576 --> 00:19:54.110
stable.

00:19:54.110 --> 00:19:54.977
That's good.

00:19:54.977 --> 00:19:57.480
But let's go to some
more examples now.

00:19:57.480 --> 00:19:59.549
Let's do a few more.

00:19:59.549 --> 00:20:01.017
All right.

00:20:01.017 --> 00:20:03.219
Now we're going to
work our way to H2.

00:20:03.219 --> 00:20:05.388
And in this case,
energy is always

00:20:05.388 --> 00:20:07.690
going up in these diagrams.

00:20:07.690 --> 00:20:11.827
Now, each of these H atoms
is bringing an electron.

00:20:11.827 --> 00:20:13.930
There's no charge to the system.

00:20:13.930 --> 00:20:20.836
And so now different
quantum numbers.

00:20:20.836 --> 00:20:21.337
Pauli.

00:20:24.740 --> 00:20:26.342
But same sigma.

00:20:26.342 --> 00:20:26.943
Right.

00:20:26.943 --> 00:20:29.078
And so now that's the H2.

00:20:29.078 --> 00:20:33.583
Now, the bond order
here is equal to 1.

00:20:33.583 --> 00:20:34.850
Right?

00:20:34.850 --> 00:20:38.387
I didn't have any electrons
in an antibonding orbital.

00:20:38.387 --> 00:20:38.888
All right.

00:20:38.888 --> 00:20:40.022
But you know what's coming.

00:20:40.022 --> 00:20:42.692
I'm going to do He2 plus next.

00:20:42.692 --> 00:20:51.534
And let's just put it here
for comparison in real time.

00:20:51.534 --> 00:20:57.773
If I were He2, each of these
would be the AO occupation

00:20:57.773 --> 00:20:59.875
of an He atom.

00:20:59.875 --> 00:21:00.376
Right?

00:21:05.548 --> 00:21:10.419
But since I'm He2 plus, one of
them is missing an electron.

00:21:10.419 --> 00:21:12.121
So this is what
you're going to see.

00:21:12.121 --> 00:21:14.657
These are both He atoms
still, but one of them

00:21:14.657 --> 00:21:18.194
is plus in this picture.

00:21:18.194 --> 00:21:19.028
But see now, OK.

00:21:19.028 --> 00:21:20.596
I've got three electrons.

00:21:20.596 --> 00:21:21.998
They're going to go here.

00:21:21.998 --> 00:21:25.434
And I've got to put one up here.

00:21:25.434 --> 00:21:26.802
I have to.

00:21:26.802 --> 00:21:27.536
Cause I had three.

00:21:27.536 --> 00:21:30.840
I got to keep on filling,
just like in an atom.

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:34.410
But now I'm filling bonding
and antibonding orbitals.

00:21:34.410 --> 00:21:34.910
All right.

00:21:34.910 --> 00:21:40.683
And here I've got
this and two up there.

00:21:40.683 --> 00:21:42.785
So if you look at
the bond order,

00:21:42.785 --> 00:21:46.622
here the bond order
is still positive.

00:21:46.622 --> 00:21:49.692
Here the bond order is zero.

00:21:49.692 --> 00:21:54.263
And, in fact, that's why He2
is not a stable molecule.

00:21:54.263 --> 00:21:57.099
Because you're putting just as
much charge on this thing that

00:21:57.099 --> 00:21:58.968
wants to bond it as
you are on this thing

00:21:58.968 --> 00:22:01.737
that doesn't want to bond it.

00:22:01.737 --> 00:22:05.141
And at the end of the day,
it leads to a molecule

00:22:05.141 --> 00:22:07.143
that is not stable.

00:22:07.143 --> 00:22:08.244
So that would be He2.

00:22:08.244 --> 00:22:10.813
But He2 plus has a bond
order that's similar.

00:22:14.150 --> 00:22:15.918
Did I get something wrong?

00:22:15.918 --> 00:22:17.553
He2 plus 1/2.

00:22:20.856 --> 00:22:21.824
OK.

00:22:21.824 --> 00:22:26.696
Now, because this weakened it.

00:22:26.696 --> 00:22:27.196
Right.

00:22:27.196 --> 00:22:29.398
This weakened the
molecule, compared

00:22:29.398 --> 00:22:31.934
to this, because
now I added charge

00:22:31.934 --> 00:22:33.336
to an antibonding orbital.

00:22:33.336 --> 00:22:35.504
So you would expect
from the bond order

00:22:35.504 --> 00:22:37.907
that this one is more
stable than this.

00:22:37.907 --> 00:22:39.975
And, in fact, that's
what you find.

00:22:39.975 --> 00:22:40.476
Right.

00:22:40.476 --> 00:22:44.647
The H2 molecule has a
stronger bonding energy.

00:22:44.647 --> 00:22:49.652
Now, we can write the molecular
orbital configurations

00:22:49.652 --> 00:22:52.955
just like we did with the
atomic orbital configurations.

00:22:52.955 --> 00:22:53.923
OK.

00:22:53.923 --> 00:23:00.029
And so like for here,
so for this case--

00:23:00.029 --> 00:23:01.931
I'm going to write it
up top if I can fit it.

00:23:01.931 --> 00:23:06.202
So here we would have sigma 1s.

00:23:06.202 --> 00:23:09.338
And we're populating it with--

00:23:09.338 --> 00:23:10.406
Ah.

00:23:10.406 --> 00:23:11.307
That's not my example.

00:23:11.307 --> 00:23:14.143
[GRUNTING]

00:23:14.143 --> 00:23:14.910
This is my example.

00:23:17.913 --> 00:23:19.749
Sigma 1s is the orbital.

00:23:19.749 --> 00:23:22.685
And we're populating
it with one electron.

00:23:22.685 --> 00:23:23.486
Right.

00:23:23.486 --> 00:23:25.321
So that's how we
would write that.

00:23:25.321 --> 00:23:28.157
Now, if we go to
the next examples,

00:23:28.157 --> 00:23:33.496
we would have this one
is this molecule you

00:23:33.496 --> 00:23:36.966
would write as sigma 1s 2.

00:23:36.966 --> 00:23:40.770
So this would be sigma 1s 2.

00:23:40.770 --> 00:23:47.076
And oh, now I've got sigma 1s 2.

00:23:47.076 --> 00:23:48.844
Oh, this is getting interesting.

00:23:48.844 --> 00:23:52.615
Because now it's
sigma star 1s 1.

00:23:52.615 --> 00:23:55.551
So everybody see that?

00:23:55.551 --> 00:23:56.519
I populate.

00:23:56.519 --> 00:23:59.088
This is just like SPD.

00:23:59.088 --> 00:24:01.824
But now I'm just
filling those MOs up.

00:24:01.824 --> 00:24:04.560
And I'm showing you what
the molecular configuration

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:08.697
is with those molecular
orbital names.

00:24:08.697 --> 00:24:09.198
Sigma 1s.

00:24:09.198 --> 00:24:10.299
Sigma 1s star.

00:24:10.299 --> 00:24:10.766
OK.

00:24:10.766 --> 00:24:11.901
Good.

00:24:11.901 --> 00:24:15.571
Now, we can keep going.

00:24:15.571 --> 00:24:19.008
We could do this all day.

00:24:19.008 --> 00:24:20.109
Actually, we kind of will.

00:24:20.109 --> 00:24:23.779
But we're going to move on--
don't worry-- from sigma.

00:24:23.779 --> 00:24:24.280
Let's see.

00:24:24.280 --> 00:24:26.882
Let me go over here.

00:24:26.882 --> 00:24:28.551
There's another
concept that I want

00:24:28.551 --> 00:24:29.952
you to know about MO theory.

00:24:34.457 --> 00:24:39.595
So if I kept going, I could
have, for example, let's

00:24:39.595 --> 00:24:40.930
do lithium.

00:24:40.930 --> 00:24:41.430
OK.

00:24:41.430 --> 00:24:44.099
So now, lithium 1s.

00:24:44.099 --> 00:24:44.867
Oh-ho.

00:24:44.867 --> 00:24:47.002
2s.

00:24:47.002 --> 00:24:49.472
Lithium brings this.

00:24:49.472 --> 00:24:50.239
What does it bring?

00:24:50.239 --> 00:24:52.441
It brings three electrons.

00:24:52.441 --> 00:25:02.551
And over here, 1s and
2s, three electrons.

00:25:02.551 --> 00:25:05.721
So this would be
the lithium dimer.

00:25:05.721 --> 00:25:07.289
But I want to make
an important point.

00:25:07.289 --> 00:25:12.728
When you would draw this one,
here's how I would draw it.

00:25:12.728 --> 00:25:17.399
Whereas when I would draw this
one, I would draw it like this.

00:25:17.399 --> 00:25:18.133
OK.

00:25:18.133 --> 00:25:20.169
Now, those are supposed
to be the same spacing.

00:25:20.169 --> 00:25:21.637
I'll tell you in a second.

00:25:24.240 --> 00:25:26.542
Notice that the distance
here between the bonding

00:25:26.542 --> 00:25:31.046
and antibonding orbital
is larger than it is here.

00:25:31.046 --> 00:25:35.618
The reason is because it's
related to the overlap.

00:25:35.618 --> 00:25:42.725
So there's another point, which
is that the overlap of AOs

00:25:42.725 --> 00:25:46.495
is related.

00:25:46.495 --> 00:25:52.535
So the greater the
overlap of AOs,

00:25:52.535 --> 00:26:01.977
the greater the change in energy
between bonding and antibonding

00:26:01.977 --> 00:26:02.478
orbitals.

00:26:14.623 --> 00:26:15.391
And let's be clear.

00:26:15.391 --> 00:26:17.693
Those are molecular.

00:26:17.693 --> 00:26:20.596
But see, you could see
like, if I'm lithium,

00:26:20.596 --> 00:26:23.999
those 1ses aren't going
to overlap too much.

00:26:23.999 --> 00:26:24.500
Right.

00:26:24.500 --> 00:26:26.502
They're kind of
close to the core.

00:26:26.502 --> 00:26:27.903
So you do get MOs there.

00:26:27.903 --> 00:26:30.606
But the difference
that you get depends

00:26:30.606 --> 00:26:35.411
on how much these electrons are
near in energy and overlapping.

00:26:35.411 --> 00:26:36.145
All right.

00:26:36.145 --> 00:26:40.249
So the 2s can
overlap a lot more.

00:26:40.249 --> 00:26:40.950
OK.

00:26:40.950 --> 00:26:42.518
Now, if I were to
fill this up, we

00:26:42.518 --> 00:26:43.786
would get something like this.

00:26:46.589 --> 00:26:53.062
These sigma 1ses-- oh, sigma 1s.

00:26:53.062 --> 00:26:56.098
What do you think
these are called?

00:26:56.098 --> 00:26:56.599
2s.

00:26:56.599 --> 00:26:58.734
Sigma 2s.

00:26:58.734 --> 00:26:59.702
Sigma 2s.

00:26:59.702 --> 00:27:01.370
Sigma 2s.

00:27:01.370 --> 00:27:01.870
OK.

00:27:01.870 --> 00:27:03.305
This is getting a little bit--

00:27:03.305 --> 00:27:04.740
I'm going to draw this out here.

00:27:04.740 --> 00:27:07.276
That's the sigma 1s star.

00:27:07.276 --> 00:27:07.776
Right.

00:27:07.776 --> 00:27:10.245
And this would be
the sigma 2s star.

00:27:10.245 --> 00:27:11.580
Well, what am I going to occupy?

00:27:11.580 --> 00:27:13.882
Well, how many electrons
do I have to pour

00:27:13.882 --> 00:27:15.250
into the molecular orbitals?

00:27:15.250 --> 00:27:17.519
[GRUNTING]

00:27:21.857 --> 00:27:27.730
Lithium 2 is stable, because
its bond order is-- you see.

00:27:27.730 --> 00:27:28.497
Bond order.

00:27:28.497 --> 00:27:30.599
Number of electrons
in bonding orbitals,

00:27:30.599 --> 00:27:33.235
one, two, three,
four, minus the number

00:27:33.235 --> 00:27:36.872
of electrons in antibonding,
minus 1, 2, divided by 2.

00:27:36.872 --> 00:27:40.976
So the lithium dimer
bonding order is 1.

00:27:40.976 --> 00:27:41.710
All right.

00:27:41.710 --> 00:27:43.812
And then you can go
on, and you could say,

00:27:43.812 --> 00:27:46.782
well, if I had beryllium, I'd
have another electron here

00:27:46.782 --> 00:27:47.449
and here.

00:27:47.449 --> 00:27:50.152
And I'd fill those
antibonding orbitals up again,

00:27:50.152 --> 00:27:52.321
like in the He2 dimer.

00:27:52.321 --> 00:27:54.356
And I would have, again,
an unstable molecule,

00:27:54.356 --> 00:27:56.191
which is, in fact, true.

00:27:56.191 --> 00:27:57.226
OK.

00:27:57.226 --> 00:28:00.629
This is sort of the
simplest way you

00:28:00.629 --> 00:28:02.297
can think about
molecular orbital theory

00:28:02.297 --> 00:28:03.832
because it's the
simplest orbital.

00:28:03.832 --> 00:28:07.469
But you could spend
the whole night.

00:28:07.469 --> 00:28:10.339
And it is a Friday.

00:28:10.339 --> 00:28:11.540
What else do you have--

00:28:11.540 --> 00:28:14.076
this is what you do on a Friday.

00:28:14.076 --> 00:28:17.780
After I watch Dr.
Quantum I'm going

00:28:17.780 --> 00:28:20.816
to just add every single one
of these with every single one.

00:28:20.816 --> 00:28:22.284
It's finite.

00:28:22.284 --> 00:28:24.920
It's not going to take forever.

00:28:24.920 --> 00:28:26.989
Let's just do p.

00:28:26.989 --> 00:28:27.589
OK.

00:28:27.589 --> 00:28:29.858
Now, with p orbitals there's
something interesting that

00:28:29.858 --> 00:28:32.261
happens, because now that sign--

00:28:32.261 --> 00:28:34.163
you see how the p has
a plus and a minus?

00:28:34.163 --> 00:28:34.663
Right.

00:28:34.663 --> 00:28:37.132
And so you've got to kind of
think about that a little bit.

00:28:40.502 --> 00:28:41.804
OK.

00:28:41.804 --> 00:28:48.444
If I take a p orbital, and
I'm going to do it like this.

00:28:48.444 --> 00:28:49.812
Minus, plus.

00:28:49.812 --> 00:28:53.949
And I'm going to add it to a p
orbital that looks like this.

00:28:53.949 --> 00:28:56.051
And notice, I'm
taking these and I'm

00:28:56.051 --> 00:28:59.755
adding them along an
axis where they're

00:28:59.755 --> 00:29:02.725
kind of both along the axis.

00:29:02.725 --> 00:29:03.692
Right.

00:29:03.692 --> 00:29:05.160
They're both along the axis.

00:29:05.160 --> 00:29:11.700
And by convention, we'll do the
pz as the one along the axis.

00:29:11.700 --> 00:29:16.105
Remember, there's
px, py, and pz.

00:29:16.105 --> 00:29:18.307
When we solve for
the p orbitals,

00:29:18.307 --> 00:29:21.410
those are the Ms. Those
are the Ms. Right.

00:29:21.410 --> 00:29:21.910
One.

00:29:21.910 --> 00:29:22.411
Zero.

00:29:22.411 --> 00:29:23.345
And minus 1.

00:29:23.345 --> 00:29:28.150
Now, by convention we put the
pz along the bonding axis.

00:29:28.150 --> 00:29:31.120
And you can see right
away, so if I do this,

00:29:31.120 --> 00:29:32.855
well, I'm going to
start adding these wave

00:29:32.855 --> 00:29:34.757
functions constructively.

00:29:34.757 --> 00:29:36.892
And what you're
going to get is--

00:29:36.892 --> 00:29:39.495
let's see if I can draw this--

00:29:39.495 --> 00:29:41.964
something that looks like that.

00:29:41.964 --> 00:29:46.502
And where this is
actually going to be plus.

00:29:46.502 --> 00:29:47.903
And this is minus.

00:29:47.903 --> 00:29:50.472
And notice that I'm going
to have nodes in there.

00:29:50.472 --> 00:29:50.973
Right.

00:29:50.973 --> 00:29:52.775
I had nodes in the
original orbitals.

00:29:52.775 --> 00:29:55.477
But I got a lot of
bonding in between.

00:29:55.477 --> 00:29:58.480
I got a lot of bonding
density in between.

00:29:58.480 --> 00:29:59.681
That is a bonding orbital.

00:30:02.484 --> 00:30:04.086
And the other thing
we see about this

00:30:04.086 --> 00:30:07.422
is that it is symmetric
around that bonding axis.

00:30:07.422 --> 00:30:11.126
So it's actually a
sigma pz orbital.

00:30:11.126 --> 00:30:12.161
It's a sigma.

00:30:12.161 --> 00:30:14.696
We call it a sigma, because it's
symmetric around the bonding

00:30:14.696 --> 00:30:15.197
axis.

00:30:15.197 --> 00:30:17.833
Now, if I were to take a px--

00:30:17.833 --> 00:30:20.736
oh, well, let's
actually subtract these.

00:30:20.736 --> 00:30:21.503
OK.

00:30:21.503 --> 00:30:23.172
So minus, plus, minus.

00:30:26.341 --> 00:30:29.778
Now, what you see is something
very different, right.

00:30:29.778 --> 00:30:32.147
So now, oh, boy.

00:30:32.147 --> 00:30:33.749
Can I draw this?

00:30:33.749 --> 00:30:35.851
Maybe.

00:30:35.851 --> 00:30:36.885
I don't know.

00:30:36.885 --> 00:30:37.820
OK.

00:30:37.820 --> 00:30:39.454
I'll take that.

00:30:39.454 --> 00:30:40.289
Right.

00:30:40.289 --> 00:30:41.690
And so now what you see--

00:30:41.690 --> 00:30:43.025
so I'm subtracting these.

00:30:43.025 --> 00:30:44.793
And so the density goes down.

00:30:44.793 --> 00:30:47.129
And now, you're going
to get minus, minus.

00:30:47.129 --> 00:30:47.696
Right.

00:30:47.696 --> 00:30:48.730
Plus, plus.

00:30:48.730 --> 00:30:50.799
But I've reduced the
electron density.

00:30:50.799 --> 00:30:53.268
And even worse, I'm
back to that situation

00:30:53.268 --> 00:30:56.638
where right in between where
I want to share the most,

00:30:56.638 --> 00:30:58.040
I'm saying no.

00:30:58.040 --> 00:31:00.275
No probability density there.

00:31:00.275 --> 00:31:03.612
And that's an antibonding
sigma p orbital.

00:31:03.612 --> 00:31:07.149
So this would be sigma star pz.

00:31:07.149 --> 00:31:07.950
OK.

00:31:07.950 --> 00:31:10.786
Sigma star pz.

00:31:10.786 --> 00:31:12.621
Now, the other thing
that you can do

00:31:12.621 --> 00:31:15.190
is look at the other directions.

00:31:15.190 --> 00:31:15.691
Right.

00:31:15.691 --> 00:31:18.060
And so I'll just take one
of those really quick.

00:31:18.060 --> 00:31:20.462
And if you do that, you
see something different.

00:31:20.462 --> 00:31:20.963
All right.

00:31:20.963 --> 00:31:23.932
So now, let's combine
these like this.

00:31:23.932 --> 00:31:27.703
By the way, some textbooks.

00:31:27.703 --> 00:31:30.138
We're adding and subtracting.

00:31:30.138 --> 00:31:34.343
We're constructively interfering
and destructively interfering.

00:31:34.343 --> 00:31:37.579
So sometimes you'll see
a textbook add like this,

00:31:37.579 --> 00:31:41.783
or add the other way, which
is subtracting like this.

00:31:41.783 --> 00:31:42.885
But it's the same thing.

00:31:42.885 --> 00:31:43.352
Right?

00:31:43.352 --> 00:31:45.254
You can decide how
you want to orient it.

00:31:45.254 --> 00:31:47.923
But we're adding and
subtracting constructively

00:31:47.923 --> 00:31:48.690
and destructively.

00:31:48.690 --> 00:31:49.758
That's what we're doing.

00:31:49.758 --> 00:31:51.560
So I'm going to choose
to add it like this.

00:31:51.560 --> 00:31:53.962
Because then you just
see, if I do this,

00:31:53.962 --> 00:31:57.599
then those are going to
have some kind of overlap.

00:31:57.599 --> 00:31:58.634
Right.

00:31:58.634 --> 00:32:01.003
Those are going to have
some overlap of plus

00:32:01.003 --> 00:32:04.439
appear and some overlap
of minus down there.

00:32:04.439 --> 00:32:08.677
And that is called,
oh, a pi orbital.

00:32:08.677 --> 00:32:09.912
Yeah.

00:32:09.912 --> 00:32:10.412
Yeah.

00:32:10.412 --> 00:32:12.848
But if I subtracted them,
then what you would see

00:32:12.848 --> 00:32:15.384
is something that looks
more like this, where you

00:32:15.384 --> 00:32:17.686
got that node in between again.

00:32:17.686 --> 00:32:18.253
Right.

00:32:18.253 --> 00:32:22.457
And this would be
a pi star orbital.

00:32:22.457 --> 00:32:24.927
This would be for
like px orbitals.

00:32:24.927 --> 00:32:28.897
Now, notice, these
cannot be sigmas,

00:32:28.897 --> 00:32:32.501
because the pi orbital is not
symmetric around the bonding

00:32:32.501 --> 00:32:33.335
axis.

00:32:33.335 --> 00:32:34.569
OK.

00:32:34.569 --> 00:32:37.306
These cannot be sigmas.

00:32:37.306 --> 00:32:39.274
But that's why we have
another symbol for them.

00:32:39.274 --> 00:32:40.776
Luckily, we've got
a lot of symbols,

00:32:40.776 --> 00:32:43.111
and the chemists are
geniuses at naming things.

00:32:43.111 --> 00:32:44.780
And so these are
called pi orbitals.

00:32:44.780 --> 00:32:47.282
And as you can see, OK.

00:32:47.282 --> 00:32:49.184
I have three orbitals.

00:32:49.184 --> 00:32:51.086
One, I put it along the axis.

00:32:51.086 --> 00:32:53.155
And then I've got two
going perpendicular

00:32:53.155 --> 00:32:54.923
in the other plane.

00:32:54.923 --> 00:32:55.924
And this is one of them.

00:32:55.924 --> 00:33:00.162
And then the other one
would be the other one, py.

00:33:00.162 --> 00:33:05.767
So as you can see, I'm going
to have a sigma star, two pi

00:33:05.767 --> 00:33:08.737
orbitals, and two
pi star orbitals.

00:33:08.737 --> 00:33:10.005
Right.

00:33:10.005 --> 00:33:12.007
And then what we got to
do is we got to put them

00:33:12.007 --> 00:33:13.942
