00:38:27.656 --> 00:38:30.358
And it's really
fun to do actually.

00:38:30.358 --> 00:38:31.493
But there you go.

00:38:31.493 --> 00:38:33.428
That's liquid nitrogen.

00:38:33.428 --> 00:38:37.999
But look at liquid
oxygen. Liquid oxygen. Now

00:38:37.999 --> 00:38:42.637
you pour it through, and the
magnet holds it in place.

00:38:42.637 --> 00:38:43.138
All right.

00:38:43.138 --> 00:38:44.039
Whoa is right.

00:38:48.343 --> 00:38:50.979
There's four more things
we've got to cover.

00:38:50.979 --> 00:38:52.380
One is paramagnetism.

00:38:58.053 --> 00:39:02.190
Another is something
called mixing.

00:39:02.190 --> 00:39:05.694
Another is what happens
when you go hetero nuclear.

00:39:05.694 --> 00:39:06.995
Oh, yeah.

00:39:06.995 --> 00:39:09.531
You'll see what that
means in a second.

00:39:09.531 --> 00:39:12.767
And finally, what I
said I would get to,

00:39:12.767 --> 00:39:16.338
which is non-bonding, which are
not the same as antibonding.

00:39:19.074 --> 00:39:24.312
What I'm covering right now,
there, is paramagnetism.

00:39:24.312 --> 00:39:27.182
Because as you saw on the
exam in one of the questions,

00:39:27.182 --> 00:39:27.849
we explained it.

00:39:27.849 --> 00:39:34.856
We said if you got unpaired
electrons, then you are--

00:39:34.856 --> 00:39:36.057
what does paramagnetism mean?

00:39:36.057 --> 00:39:39.627
It means that if you put a huge
magnet on it, you'll respond.

00:39:39.627 --> 00:39:43.665
You will feel a force
from that magnetic field.

00:39:43.665 --> 00:39:44.933
And so we did that in the exam.

00:39:44.933 --> 00:39:47.268
We had a silicon atom, because
we hadn't done molecules.

00:39:47.268 --> 00:39:48.703
But now we got molecules.

00:39:48.703 --> 00:39:51.039
So I can tell you why
that experiment happened.

00:39:51.039 --> 00:39:52.340
What's happening?

00:39:52.340 --> 00:39:55.310
I can tell you why that
experiment happens.

00:39:55.310 --> 00:39:55.810
Really?

00:39:55.810 --> 00:39:57.212
I didn't even know
there was more.

00:39:57.212 --> 00:39:59.481
[LAUGHTER]

00:39:59.481 --> 00:40:03.518
It happens because of Hund's
rule, molecular orbitals,

00:40:03.518 --> 00:40:06.121
and the fact that I've got
these two unpaired electrons

00:40:06.121 --> 00:40:08.590
in the O2 molecule.

00:40:08.590 --> 00:40:11.993
And you know that in nitrogen
those two electrons are gone,

00:40:11.993 --> 00:40:14.596
cause nitrogen is missing
one more here and here.

00:40:14.596 --> 00:40:15.730
So you've got two less.

00:40:15.730 --> 00:40:18.433
Everything's filled.

00:40:18.433 --> 00:40:19.667
Everything's filled.

00:40:19.667 --> 00:40:22.404
Paramagnetism.

00:40:22.404 --> 00:40:23.872
Now, OK.

00:40:23.872 --> 00:40:26.408
Speaking of magnetism,
I couldn't help but show

00:40:26.408 --> 00:40:28.076
you this.

00:40:28.076 --> 00:40:29.077
This is diamagnetism.

00:40:29.077 --> 00:40:31.212
Now, diamagnetism,
everything is filled.

00:40:31.212 --> 00:40:34.082
But you still feel a little
repulsion to a magnetic field.

00:40:34.082 --> 00:40:36.484
And some of you may know there
is this thing called the Ig

00:40:36.484 --> 00:40:37.385
Noble Prize.

00:40:37.385 --> 00:40:39.387
Only one person
in history has won

00:40:39.387 --> 00:40:42.223
both the Ig Noble
and the Nobel Prize,

00:40:42.223 --> 00:40:45.493
Andre Geim who discovered
graphene with scotch tape.

00:40:45.493 --> 00:40:47.262
But before that he
won the Ig Noble Prize

00:40:47.262 --> 00:40:50.031
because he did this to frogs.

00:40:50.031 --> 00:40:53.835
Because water is diamagnetic.

00:40:53.835 --> 00:40:55.336
And so it repels
a magnetic field.

00:40:55.336 --> 00:40:58.907
It's just got to be really,
really, really high.

00:40:58.907 --> 00:41:00.909
I hope that frog was OK.

00:41:00.909 --> 00:41:02.110
It looked sort of OK.

00:41:02.110 --> 00:41:03.645
So the frog was floating.

00:41:03.645 --> 00:41:06.815
And it was like a study about
levitation using magnetism.

00:41:06.815 --> 00:41:08.049
Why am I showing that to you?

00:41:08.049 --> 00:41:09.117
No particular reason.

00:41:09.117 --> 00:41:11.019
[LAUGHTER]

00:41:11.019 --> 00:41:12.921
But this does get me to
the why this matters,

00:41:12.921 --> 00:41:14.889
which has to do with
how you cook pasta.

00:41:14.889 --> 00:41:22.831
And, of course, since
we're talking about O2,

00:41:22.831 --> 00:41:27.068
when I have finished
cooking pasta, what do I do?

00:41:27.068 --> 00:41:28.136
I pour it.

00:41:28.136 --> 00:41:28.803
There it is.

00:41:28.803 --> 00:41:30.638
It's like it's sophisticated.

00:41:30.638 --> 00:41:32.707
I pour it through a colander.

00:41:32.707 --> 00:41:34.943
That's a membrane.

00:41:34.943 --> 00:41:35.710
That's a membrane.

00:41:35.710 --> 00:41:36.845
You did use a membrane.

00:41:36.845 --> 00:41:38.012
You did a filter.

00:41:38.012 --> 00:41:40.982
Now, but I could also
have done that separation,

00:41:40.982 --> 00:41:44.619
that same separation, I could
have left it on the stove.

00:41:44.619 --> 00:41:46.221
I could have.

00:41:46.221 --> 00:41:48.356
And it would have
boiled out all the water

00:41:48.356 --> 00:41:51.960
and left me still with the
pasta separated from the water.

00:41:51.960 --> 00:41:54.195
I've accomplished
the same exact thing.

00:41:54.195 --> 00:41:56.164
I have separated the
pasta from the water.

00:41:56.164 --> 00:41:58.132
Test done.

00:41:58.132 --> 00:42:00.034
Pasta may not taste as good.

00:42:00.034 --> 00:42:01.302
[LAUGHTER]

00:42:01.302 --> 00:42:02.103
But you've done it.

00:42:02.103 --> 00:42:04.973
But see, the thing is that if
you separate things this way

00:42:04.973 --> 00:42:08.142
versus that way, you can just
feel how much less energy

00:42:08.142 --> 00:42:08.943
it's going to take.

00:42:08.943 --> 00:42:11.513
In fact, you can save
over 80% of the energy

00:42:11.513 --> 00:42:13.748
if you do a membrane
based separation

00:42:13.748 --> 00:42:16.050
as opposed to a thermal one.

00:42:16.050 --> 00:42:17.185
Gazuntite.

00:42:17.185 --> 00:42:18.853
So those are two ways
to separate pasta.

00:42:18.853 --> 00:42:21.756
But see, there's two ways
to separate lots of things.

00:42:21.756 --> 00:42:24.392
Like how about 1 to 10
nanometer particles?

00:42:24.392 --> 00:42:25.493
How about chemistry?

00:42:25.493 --> 00:42:27.228
How about molecules?

00:42:27.228 --> 00:42:29.697
How do you separate those?

00:42:29.697 --> 00:42:31.332
Well, you got the same two ways.

00:42:31.332 --> 00:42:35.136
And if you count up all the
things we separate this way,

00:42:35.136 --> 00:42:36.004
it's a lot.

00:42:36.004 --> 00:42:37.071
It goes on and on.

00:42:37.071 --> 00:42:39.474
And it will go all the way
down the Infinite Corridor.

00:42:39.474 --> 00:42:41.442
And this is how we do chemistry.

00:42:41.442 --> 00:42:46.281
In fact, has anyone seen
this on the side of the road?

00:42:46.281 --> 00:42:47.615
That's a distillation column.

00:42:47.615 --> 00:42:50.018
It's a big pasta cooker.

00:42:50.018 --> 00:42:53.621
That's all you're doing is
boiling one molecule off

00:42:53.621 --> 00:42:59.694
of another over a long time
with a whole lot of fossil fuel.

00:42:59.694 --> 00:43:01.996
In fact, if you look at
the US energy consumption,

00:43:01.996 --> 00:43:04.299
roughly a third of it
goes into industry.

00:43:04.299 --> 00:43:06.868
40% of that is for
this one thing.

00:43:06.868 --> 00:43:08.002
It's boiling pasta.

00:43:08.002 --> 00:43:12.206
But the pasta is 1 nanometer
to 10 nanometer particles.

00:43:12.206 --> 00:43:15.543
40% goes into boiling
one chemical species off

00:43:15.543 --> 00:43:16.144
of another.

00:43:16.144 --> 00:43:18.179
Separation.

00:43:18.179 --> 00:43:20.415
Separation.

00:43:20.415 --> 00:43:23.384
That's 12% of all the energy.

00:43:23.384 --> 00:43:25.853
That's the same as
every single drop

00:43:25.853 --> 00:43:29.257
of gasoline in every
single car truck and bus.

00:43:29.257 --> 00:43:31.426
Just to give you a sense
of how much energy that is.

00:43:31.426 --> 00:43:34.495
You'll say, well, why
aren't we using a colander?

00:43:34.495 --> 00:43:36.331
Why don't we just pour
it through a colander

00:43:36.331 --> 00:43:38.032
like we do our boiling pasta?

00:43:38.032 --> 00:43:39.834
Well, we do that for one field.

00:43:39.834 --> 00:43:40.835
Desalination.

00:43:40.835 --> 00:43:43.171
I got a [INAUDIBLE]
on that another time.

00:43:43.171 --> 00:43:45.273
But we don't do it for
all those other things.

00:43:45.273 --> 00:43:46.741
And there's a really
simple reason.

00:43:46.741 --> 00:43:50.511
We don't have the
right pasta colander.

00:43:50.511 --> 00:43:51.913
We don't.

00:43:51.913 --> 00:43:55.283
There's no option
for that size scale

00:43:55.283 --> 00:43:59.420
that can withstand the
conditions that are in all

00:43:59.420 --> 00:44:01.356
of those chemical separations.

00:44:01.356 --> 00:44:04.258
But if we did--

00:44:04.258 --> 00:44:08.429
we take so much
O2 out of the air.

00:44:08.429 --> 00:44:09.497
We need O2.

00:44:09.497 --> 00:44:11.065
But we don't want the N2.

00:44:11.065 --> 00:44:12.734
So we have to separate it.

00:44:12.734 --> 00:44:13.735
How do we do it?

00:44:13.735 --> 00:44:14.969
We go cryogenic.

00:44:14.969 --> 00:44:16.537
We go to very, very
cold temperatures,

00:44:16.537 --> 00:44:18.640
which is the same as boiling.

00:44:18.640 --> 00:44:19.574
Right.

00:44:19.574 --> 00:44:21.509
But you're still spending
all this fossil fuel

00:44:21.509 --> 00:44:23.244
to lower the temperature.

00:44:23.244 --> 00:44:26.514
That's how much O2 we
generate each year.

00:44:26.514 --> 00:44:28.082
And this is how much
energy it takes.

00:44:28.082 --> 00:44:31.285
It's like 1/2 a percent of
all US energy, just to get--

00:44:31.285 --> 00:44:34.455
But what if you
could use something

00:44:34.455 --> 00:44:36.524
like O2's paramagnetism?

00:44:36.524 --> 00:44:39.927
What if you could use something
about the chemistry or O2

00:44:39.927 --> 00:44:43.431
to do this separation
more efficiently,

00:44:43.431 --> 00:44:47.635
lower energy, or maybe make a
new colander that does that?

00:44:47.635 --> 00:44:50.672
And if any of you have
ideas, come talk to me.

00:44:50.672 --> 00:44:54.042
This is a problem
I care a lot about.

00:44:54.042 --> 00:44:55.243
OK.

00:44:55.243 --> 00:44:55.743
Ah.

00:44:55.743 --> 00:44:57.345
But I had some
other-- paramagnetism.

00:44:57.345 --> 00:44:58.279
Unpaired electrons.

00:44:58.279 --> 00:44:59.514
We got that one.

00:44:59.514 --> 00:45:01.983
Ha.

00:45:01.983 --> 00:45:02.950
[SIGHS]

00:45:02.950 --> 00:45:04.952
Why is chemistry not--

00:45:04.952 --> 00:45:07.555
why can't they follow the rules?

00:45:07.555 --> 00:45:08.322
Why?

00:45:08.322 --> 00:45:10.992
But they always
got to break them.

00:45:10.992 --> 00:45:14.962
And what we see, this
was oxygen. Sigma.

00:45:14.962 --> 00:45:16.964
Sigma S. Sigma.

00:45:16.964 --> 00:45:17.832
Sigma star.

00:45:17.832 --> 00:45:18.332
Pi.

00:45:18.332 --> 00:45:19.133
Pi star.

00:45:19.133 --> 00:45:23.071
But look at what happens
for nitrogen. Why?

00:45:23.071 --> 00:45:26.641
Because of something some
people like to call mixing.

00:45:26.641 --> 00:45:29.777
Remember, I said that--

00:45:29.777 --> 00:45:35.850
where did it-- somewhere I
said that the closer in energy,

00:45:35.850 --> 00:45:38.720
or the closer in
symmetry orbitals are,

00:45:38.720 --> 00:45:40.755
the more overlap they can
have, and the more they

00:45:40.755 --> 00:45:43.391
interact and can mix
together in the ways

00:45:43.391 --> 00:45:44.759
that I've been talking about.

00:45:44.759 --> 00:45:45.460
Yeah.

00:45:45.460 --> 00:45:48.863
Well, it turns out that if
you go below oxygen, in what

00:45:48.863 --> 00:45:51.332
are called homonuclear dimers,
which is where the atoms are

00:45:51.332 --> 00:45:54.368
the same, then you
can get mixing even

00:45:54.368 --> 00:45:58.673
between this sigma
and that sigma.

00:45:58.673 --> 00:46:00.308
And so what happens
is, instead of them

00:46:00.308 --> 00:46:02.643
being kind of separate
like this for N2,

00:46:02.643 --> 00:46:04.812
there is an interaction.

00:46:04.812 --> 00:46:05.580
You see.

00:46:05.580 --> 00:46:07.081
You can think about
it the same way.

00:46:07.081 --> 00:46:09.317
I'm throwing more
orbitals into the mix.

00:46:09.317 --> 00:46:12.820
And so because they can
contribute to overlapping,

00:46:12.820 --> 00:46:15.123
you're changing that
delta E even more.

00:46:15.123 --> 00:46:17.892
That's effectively
what's happening.

00:46:17.892 --> 00:46:21.062
And so you can see this
one for nitrogen goes down,

00:46:21.062 --> 00:46:22.830
but that one goes up.

00:46:22.830 --> 00:46:26.134
Because that's the delta E.
Because it's able to mix in.

00:46:26.134 --> 00:46:28.469
It's able to mix in because
they have the same symmetry.

00:46:28.469 --> 00:46:29.637
And they're closer.

00:46:29.637 --> 00:46:33.775
For those smaller atoms,
they're a lot closer in energy.

00:46:33.775 --> 00:46:38.112
And so if you look at this,
what happens is those switch.

00:46:38.112 --> 00:46:38.646
They switch.

00:46:38.646 --> 00:46:39.881
It's real.

00:46:39.881 --> 00:46:40.381
They switch.

00:46:40.381 --> 00:46:42.583
Now, it doesn't change the
thing I just talked about,

00:46:42.583 --> 00:46:44.218
which is the magnetic
properties of N2,

00:46:44.218 --> 00:46:47.054
because they are
still all filled.

00:46:47.054 --> 00:46:48.389
But it is important.

00:46:48.389 --> 00:46:51.659
Because if I were to pull
an electron out of N2,

00:46:51.659 --> 00:46:55.563
it would come from a sigma
orbital, not a pi orbital,

00:46:55.563 --> 00:46:56.831
because of those interactions.

00:46:56.831 --> 00:46:58.800
And those happen below O2.

00:46:58.800 --> 00:47:00.468
So this is from your textbook.

00:47:00.468 --> 00:47:03.638
And you see, it says
2s 2pz interaction.

00:47:03.638 --> 00:47:04.572
Remember, 2pz is sigma.

00:47:04.572 --> 00:47:05.239
Sigma.

00:47:05.239 --> 00:47:06.908
Same symmetry.

00:47:06.908 --> 00:47:08.276
Able to mix.

00:47:08.276 --> 00:47:09.944
Don't mix very well here.

00:47:09.944 --> 00:47:12.747
But here they're
strong enough to change

00:47:12.747 --> 00:47:16.517
the ordering of that
orbital and that orbital.

00:47:16.517 --> 00:47:17.018
Right.

00:47:17.018 --> 00:47:18.920
You see that?

00:47:18.920 --> 00:47:20.888
That's an important effect.

00:47:20.888 --> 00:47:22.056
OK.

00:47:22.056 --> 00:47:22.557
All right.

00:47:25.092 --> 00:47:27.094
But it only happens below O2.

00:47:27.094 --> 00:47:29.063
It happens below
O2, because that's

00:47:29.063 --> 00:47:31.999
where those energies
and orbitals can line up

00:47:31.999 --> 00:47:34.669
in that way.

00:47:34.669 --> 00:47:35.169
OK.

00:47:35.169 --> 00:47:36.370
So we just covered that.

00:47:36.370 --> 00:47:37.605
Now, there's two more things.

00:47:40.975 --> 00:47:43.477
And then that's
all of MO theory.

00:47:43.477 --> 00:47:46.647
One is I've been giving
you the same atoms.

00:47:46.647 --> 00:47:49.750
But what if we go from
a homonuclear dimer

00:47:49.750 --> 00:47:52.286
to a heteronuclear dimer, which
just means one is one and one

00:47:52.286 --> 00:47:54.589
is another type of atom.

00:47:54.589 --> 00:47:58.860
How do we draw an
MO diagram for that?

00:47:58.860 --> 00:48:02.597
And the second thing
is what happens?

00:48:02.597 --> 00:48:05.733
The second thing is what
happens in this case?

00:48:05.733 --> 00:48:11.505
In HCl, H is bringing only
one S electron to the party.

00:48:11.505 --> 00:48:13.374
But Cl is bringing
all of the-- it's

00:48:13.374 --> 00:48:17.211
bringing S. It's bringing
P. What does it do?

00:48:17.211 --> 00:48:17.812
Right.

00:48:17.812 --> 00:48:20.348
How does the MO diagram
look in that case, where

00:48:20.348 --> 00:48:22.183
I've got all these
extra electrons coming

00:48:22.183 --> 00:48:24.518
in from one of the atoms.

00:48:24.518 --> 00:48:26.988
Now, I will not do
this in 30 seconds.

00:48:26.988 --> 00:48:30.858
But I will, next week,
give you a nice sort

00:48:30.858 --> 00:48:35.229
of thorough explanation for
each of these two last MO cases.

00:48:35.229 --> 00:48:37.331
In the meantime, have
a very good weekend.

